{"name":"Mulberry Silk","hex":"#94766c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mulberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several trees, of the genus Morus, having edible fruits.","The fruit of this tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark purple color tinted with red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mulberry"},{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk","wikidata":"Q37681"},{"title":"Morus (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Morus is a genus of flowering plants in the family Moraceae, consisting of 17 species of deciduous trees commonly known as mulberries, growing wild and under cultivation in many warm-temperate world regions. Generally, the genus has 64 subordinate taxa; although the three of most common are referred to as white, red, and black, originating from the colour of their dormant buds, and not necessarily the fruit colour, with numerous cultivars and some taxa currently unchecked and awaiting taxonomic scrutiny. Morus alba is native to China, but is widely cultivated and often naturalised across Europe, Southern Africa, South America, and North America; it is also the species most preferred by the silkworm. It is regarded as an invasive species in Brazil, the United States and some states of Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg/330px-Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morus_(plant)","wikidata":"Q44789"},{"title":"Mulberry (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The colour mulberry is a representation of the colour of mulberry jam or pie, a darker colour than that of the fresh fruit.\nTerms describing interchangeable shades, with overlapping RGB ranges, include burgundy, claret and maroon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Morus_alba_fruits.jpg/330px-Morus_alba_fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_(color)","wikidata":"Q218218"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg/500px-Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg/500px-Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Four of the most important domesticated silk moths. Top to bottom: B. mori, Hyalophora cecropia, Antheraea pernyi, Samia cynthia.From Meyers Konversations-Lexikon (1885–1892)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeyers_b14_s0826a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Eri_silk_worm.jpg/500px-Eri_silk_worm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Eri_silk_worm.jpg/1280px-Eri_silk_worm.jpg?u